          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use paint thinner on latex paint?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Latex and acrylic paints are water-based, and solvents like paint thinner or mineral spirits do not mix with them properly. They can curdle the paint or weaken the film. Thin latex only with water, within the paint maker's limit, or with a latex conditioner such as Floetrol. Clean latex brushes with warm soapy water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is turpentine better than mineral spirits?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Turpentine is a stronger solvent and the traditional choice for artist oils, but for house painting it is rarely better. It smells stronger, has high VOC content, costs more and is more irritating to skin. Odorless mineral spirits thin oil paint and clean brushes perfectly well. Choose real gum turpentine when a specific recipe or restoration calls for it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
