        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Match the Solvent to the Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paint thinner is not one product. It is a job, and the right liquid depends entirely on what is in the can you are thinning. Oil-based and alkyd paints, oil stains and oil varnishes thin and clean up with mineral spirits. Latex and acrylic paints thin with water or a latex conditioner such as Floetrol, and nothing from the solvent aisle belongs in them. Lacquer thins with lacquer thinner. Shellac thins with denatured alcohol. Get that matching right and most of the problems people blame on bad paint, from gummy brushes to wrinkled finishes, simply disappear.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners the default purchase is a can of odorless mineral spirits. It thins oil paint and varnish, cleans brushes and rollers, wipes off fresh drips and works as a degreaser before painting. It costs less per job than turpentine, smells far less than regular mineral spirits or the cheap jug labeled paint thinner, and is sold in quart and gallon cans at every hardware store. Klean-Strip Odorless Mineral Spirits is our top pick because it is everywhere, the label is clear about what it does, and the maker sells a separate formula for California and other low-VOC areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two of our picks are not solvents at all, and that is deliberate. Floetrol and Penetrol are paint conditioners from Flood, now part of PPG. They improve flow and leveling, extend open time and reduce brush marks without diluting the paint the way water or spirits do. If your real goal is a smoother finish rather than a thinner one, a conditioner usually gets you there with less risk of runs, poor hiding or a coat that fails to meet the paint maker's film-thickness spec.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Quick Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the cleanup line on the paint can first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If it says soap and water, you need water or Floetrol, not thinner. If it says mineral spirits, buy mineral spirits. The paint maker has already told you which solvent its product was designed around, and the can is the only source that knows the exact formula.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a lab test of these products. We built this list from manufacturer product pages, can labels, technical data sheets and safety data sheets, plus the patterns that show up again and again in owner reports. For each product we checked what the maker says it is for, which paints and finishes it is approved to thin, the mixing ratios printed on the label, VOC content where published, whether a California or South Coast compliant version exists, and what the maker recommends for storage and disposal.
          </p>

          <p>
