        <div id="thinner-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Paint Thinner vs Mineral Spirits vs Turpentine vs Acetone
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mineral spirits and the product sold as paint thinner both come from petroleum distillation, and in the US they do the same job. The difference is refinement. Mineral spirits are more refined, which means less odor and fewer of the lighter, harsher compounds. Products labeled paint thinner are typically a less refined cut, sometimes blended, and they smell stronger. Odorless mineral spirits go a step further, with most of the aromatic hydrocarbons removed. That makes them gentler to breathe and slightly slower to evaporate, which painters often consider a bonus because it gives the paint more time to level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turpentine is a different material entirely. Real gum spirits of turpentine is distilled from pine resin. It is a stronger solvent than mineral spirits, it has a sharp pine smell, and it is the traditional thinner for artist oil colors and old-recipe finishes. It is also more of an irritant, and some people develop skin sensitivity to it with repeated contact. For house painting there is little reason to choose turpentine over mineral spirits unless an old formula or a restoration recipe calls for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acetone and lacquer thinner sit at the aggressive end. Acetone evaporates in seconds and dissolves many plastics, adhesives and some cured finishes. Lacquer thinner is a blend of fast solvents built to dissolve and thin nitrocellulose lacquer. Pour either into an alkyd or oil paint and you risk curdling it, and brushing them over a fresh oil coat can lift or wrinkle the film. They are cleanup and removal tools for specific jobs, which is why they belong alongside an <a href="/best-adhesive-remover/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">adhesive remover</a> in the toolbox, not in a paint bucket.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
