          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are paint thinner and mineral spirits the same thing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do the same job but are not identical. Both are petroleum distillates that thin oil-based paint and clean brushes. Mineral spirits are more refined, with less odor and fewer harsh compounds; products labeled paint thinner are typically a less refined cut. Odorless mineral spirits go further, removing most aromatics. For oil paint, they are interchangeable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use paint thinner on latex paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Latex and acrylic paints are water-based, and solvents like paint thinner or mineral spirits do not mix with them properly. They can curdle the paint or weaken the film. Thin latex only with water, within the paint maker's limit, or with a latex conditioner such as Floetrol. Clean latex brushes with warm soapy water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is turpentine better than mineral spirits?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Turpentine is a stronger solvent and the traditional choice for artist oils, but for house painting it is rarely better. It smells stronger, has high VOC content, costs more and is more irritating to skin. Odorless mineral spirits thin oil paint and clean brushes perfectly well. Choose real gum turpentine when a specific recipe or restoration calls for it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much thinner should I add to paint for a sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with the paint's technical data sheet and the sprayer's manual, not a generic ratio. Many airless sprayers spray house paint unthinned. If you need flow help, Flood allows up to 32 ounces of Floetrol per gallon of latex for airless spraying, and Penetrol starts at about one pint per gallon for oil paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use acetone as a paint thinner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for house paint. Acetone evaporates in seconds, can curdle oil-based paint and dissolves many plastics. It is useful for removing dried residue, cleaning some tools and certain specialty coatings that list it on the label. For thinning oil-based paint, use mineral spirits; for latex, use water or Floetrol.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I dispose of paint thinner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Never pour it down a drain, toilet or storm drain, or onto the ground. Let used thinner settle in a sealed jar, pour off and reuse the clear solvent, and let the sludge dry out. Take leftover liquid and sludge in labeled containers to a household hazardous waste site or collection day in your area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is the mineral spirits in California different?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                California and some other states limit VOCs in consumer solvents, so traditional mineral spirits cannot be sold there. Makers sell reformulated versions under the same brand. Klean-Strip says its CARB formula dries slower, and some compliant products are milky or water-based. Read the label and dry times every time you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

